Portfolio Builder — System Strategy & Technical Architecture Document
1. Executive Summary
This document defines the complete technical workflow, database architecture, API designs, state management flow, payment verification pipeline, and UI/UX implementation strategy for the Portfolio Builder Platform.


The platform is designed using a Next.js frontend and a Node.js + Express + Mongoose (MongoDB) backend ecosystem.


________________


2. Full User Journey & User Experience (UX) Flow
Step 1: Landing / Dashboard
* User lands on the portfolio builder landing page.
* Primary CTA button displayed: "Create Portfolio".
Step 2: Theme Selection & Editor Entry
* Clicking "Create Portfolio" redirects the user to the /editor page.
* The editor loads a dynamic modal/grid displaying available portfolio themes with:
   * Theme Thumbnail Image & Badges
   * Theme Name, Description, and Price ($ or BDT)
   * Hover Action Card with two clear buttons:
      1. "Preview": Opens a live sandbox preview of the selected theme in a modal or new tab.
      2. "Buy Theme": Triggers the checkout pipeline.
Step 3: Checkout & Payment Gateway Integration
* Clicking "Buy" sends a request to the backend API (/api/v1/payments/create-checkout-session) to generate a secure payment session URL (Stripe, SSLCommerz, Shurjopay, or BKash).
* User is redirected to the external secure payment gateway page.
Step 4: Verification & Automated Editor Access
* Upon successful payment, the gateway redirects to /payment/success?session_id={SESSION_ID}.
* Next.js frontend calls /api/v1/payments/verify or listens to secure Webhooks from the payment provider.
* Backend verifies transaction, marks theme ownership in MongoDB (UserThemePurchase collection), and provisions an unlocked workspace.
* User is automatically redirected back to /editor?theme={THEME_ID}&status=unlocked with full editing capabilities enabled.


